  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively provide relevant but fragmented directions: arc melting and fast cooling or water quenching can retain metastable HEA phases; compositional changes can promote a Laves-containing two-phase structure; and prior work links magnetic species or spin fluctuations to phase behavior. Several records also describe C14 or other Laves phases coexisting with a matrix and as-cast chemical segregation. However, none studies the FeCoNiTi system, an fcc–C14 magnetic composite, the matched C14-dominant comparison, sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, orbital-moment-assisted coercivity, spin canting, or domain-wall pinning. Thus, the literature points toward quench-controlled multiphase alloy design but would require substantial new insight to predict the specific intervention and high-temperature coercivity mechanism."
